-- status  : Open
-- author  : @mikedeng1
-- created : 2026-10-09T21:03:23.949974+00:00
-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/fa0f29a7-c8c7-4301-bff8-5524b7ba30fd
-- title:
--   Theorem 5.3, pp. 20–21 — informativity for quadratic stabilization iff the reduced LMIs (5.9a)–(5.9b) in (P, β) are feasible; (5.11a)–(5.11b) under Φ₂₂ < 0; explicit gain (5.10)
-- statement:
--   Let $\Phi\in\boldsymbol\Pi_{n,T}$ and consider data $(U_-, X)$ generated by $x(t+1)=A_sx(t)+B_su(t)+w(t)$ with noise model $W_-^\top\in\mathcal Z_T(\Phi)$. Define $\Theta := \Phi_{12} + X_+\Phi_{22}$ and $V := \begin{bmatrix} X_-\\ U_-\end{bmatrix}$.
--
--   1. The data are informative for quadratic stabilization if and only if there exist an $n\times n$ matrix $P>0$ and a scalar $\beta>0$ such that
--   $$P-\beta I-[I\ \ X_+]\,\Phi\begin{bmatrix} I\\ X_+^\top\end{bmatrix} + \Theta V^\top\big(V\Phi_{22}V^\top\big)^\dagger V\Theta^\top\ge 0, \tag{5.9a}$$
--   $$\begin{bmatrix} P-\beta I&0\\ 0&-P\end{bmatrix} - \begin{bmatrix} I&X_+\\ 0&-X_-\end{bmatrix}\Phi\begin{bmatrix} I&X_+\\ 0&-X_-\end{bmatrix}^\top\ge 0. \tag{5.9b}$$
--   2. If $P>0$ and $\beta>0$ satisfy (5.9a) and (5.9b), then
--   $$K = \big(U_-(\Phi_{22}+\Theta^\top\Gamma^\dagger\Theta)X_-^\top\big)\big(X_-(\Phi_{22}+\Theta^\top\Gamma^\dagger\Theta)X_-^\top\big)^\dagger,\qquad \Gamma = P-\beta I-[I\ \ X_+]\,\Phi\begin{bmatrix} I\\ X_+^\top\end{bmatrix}, \tag{5.10}$$
--   is a stabilizing gain for all $(A,B)\in\Sigma$.
--   3. Assume in addition that $\Phi_{22}<0$ and $\operatorname{rank} V = n+m$. Then the data are informative for quadratic stabilization if and only if there exists an $n\times n$ matrix $P>0$ with
--   $$P-[I\ \ X_+]\,\Phi\begin{bmatrix} I\\ X_+^\top\end{bmatrix} + \Theta V^\top\big(V\Phi_{22}V^\top\big)^{-1} V\Theta^\top > 0, \tag{5.11a}$$
--   $$\begin{bmatrix} P&0\\ 0&-P\end{bmatrix} - \begin{bmatrix} I&X_+\\ 0&-X_-\end{bmatrix}\Phi\begin{bmatrix} I&X_+\\ 0&-X_-\end{bmatrix}^\top > 0. \tag{5.11b}$$
--   4. Under the assumptions of 3, if $P>0$ satisfies (5.11a) and (5.11b), then $K$ in (5.10), now with $\Gamma = P-[I\ \ X_+]\Phi[I\ \ X_+]^\top$, is a stabilizing feedback gain for all $(A,B)\in\Sigma$.
--
--   In items 2 and 4, "$K$ is a stabilizing gain for all $(A,B)\in\Sigma$" means: for every $(A,B)\in\Sigma$, $P-(A+BK)P(A+BK)^\top>0$ with the same $P$, and $(A+BK)^k\to 0$.
--
--   Compared with Theorem 5.1, the LMI no longer contains the controller variable $L$: one first computes a Lyapunov matrix $P$ from an LMI of size $4n+1$, and then obtains a controller from the closed-form expression (5.10).
--
--   **Formalization Note** "Generated by (2.1) with noise model (2.3)" is the hypothesis that $\Sigma$ contains some pair $(A_s,B_s)$. The Lyapunov inequality is asserted with the $P$ of the reduced LMI, which is what the proof delivers via Theorem 5.1. Pseudo-inverses are the Moore–Penrose inverse of the mission's definitions file; the inverse in (5.11a) is the ordinary inverse, which exists under the assumptions of part (b).
-- source:
--   van Waarde–Camlibel–Eising–Trentelman, arXiv:2203.12959v3, Theorem 5.3, pp. 20–21, (5.9a)–(5.11b), (5.10)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_QuadMatIneq_Reduced_QMI
import Definitions.Def_QuadMatIneq_Reduced_Data
open Matrix

namespace QuadMatIneq.Reduced

theorem theorem_5_3 {n m T : ℕ} (Φ : Matrix (Fin n ⊕ Fin T) (Fin n ⊕ Fin T) ℝ)
    (X : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin (T + 1)) ℝ) (Um : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin T) ℝ)
    (hΦ : InPi Φ) (hgen : ∃ As Bs, (As, Bs) ∈ QuadMatIneq.Stabilization.Sigma Φ X Um) :
    -- (a)
    (QuadMatIneq.Stabilization.InformativeQS Φ X Um ↔
      ∃ (P : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) (β : ℝ), P.PosDef ∧ 0 < β ∧
        (lmi59a Φ X Um P β).PosSemidef ∧ (lmi59b Φ X P β).PosSemidef) ∧
    -- Moreover of (a): the gain (5.10) with Γ = P − βI − [I X₊]Φ[I; X₊ᵀ]
    (∀ (P : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) (β : ℝ), P.PosDef → 0 < β →
      (lmi59a Φ X Um P β).PosSemidef → (lmi59b Φ X P β).PosSemidef →
        ∀ AB ∈ QuadMatIneq.Stabilization.Sigma Φ X Um,
          (P - (AB.1 + AB.2 * gain510 Φ X Um (Gamma Φ X P β)) * P *
              (AB.1 + AB.2 * gain510 Φ X Um (Gamma Φ X P β))ᵀ).PosDef ∧
          Filter.Tendsto (fun k : ℕ => (AB.1 + AB.2 * gain510 Φ X Um (Gamma Φ X P β)) ^ k)
            Filter.atTop (nhds 0)) ∧
    -- (b)
    ((-Φ.toBlocks₂₂).PosDef → (Matrix.fromRows (QuadMatIneq.Stabilization.Xminus X) Um).rank = n + m →
      (QuadMatIneq.Stabilization.InformativeQS Φ X Um ↔
        ∃ P : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ, P.PosDef ∧
          (lmi511a Φ X Um P).PosDef ∧ (lmi511b Φ X P).PosDef)) ∧
    -- Moreover of (b): the gain (5.10) with Γ = P − [I X₊]Φ[I; X₊ᵀ]
    ((-Φ.toBlocks₂₂).PosDef → (Matrix.fromRows (QuadMatIneq.Stabilization.Xminus X) Um).rank = n + m →
      ∀ P : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ, P.PosDef →
        (lmi511a Φ X Um P).PosDef → (lmi511b Φ X P).PosDef →
          ∀ AB ∈ QuadMatIneq.Stabilization.Sigma Φ X Um,
            (P - (AB.1 + AB.2 * gain510 Φ X Um (GammaB Φ X P)) * P *
                (AB.1 + AB.2 * gain510 Φ X Um (GammaB Φ X P))ᵀ).PosDef ∧
            Filter.Tendsto (fun k : ℕ => (AB.1 + AB.2 * gain510 Φ X Um (GammaB Φ X P)) ^ k)
              Filter.atTop (nhds 0)) := by sorry

end QuadMatIneq.Reduced
